<p>Kranjska Gora is a natural base for dog owners: forest roads, valley walks and mountain scenery begin almost immediately. The trap is assuming that because a dog loves walking, every alpine route is automatically a good dog route.</p>
<h2>Dogs in Triglav National Park</h2>
<p>Kranjska Gora’s official visitor guidance for Triglav National Park says dogs should be kept on a leash in the natural environment. Visitors are also asked to stay on marked routes and keep dogs away from livestock.</p>
<p>Dogs are not allowed to swim in high-mountain lakes and streams inside the protected environment. That rule matters especially on warm days, when owners may otherwise plan around water access that is not available.</p>
<h2>Choose terrain before distance</h2>
<p>A five-kilometre rocky climb can be much harder on paws and joints than a longer shaded valley walk. Think about surface, sun exposure, steep descents, water availability and how easily you can turn back.</p>
<h2>Transport can change your plan</h2>
<p>Seasonal transport in the area may have dog restrictions. For example, 2026 visitor information for the Vrata Valley shuttle specifies that small dogs in travel bags are allowed. Do not assume a large dog can board every local bus or shuttle.</p>
<div class="noteBox"><strong>Nani rule:</strong> the best dog adventure is the one the dog would choose again tomorrow.</div>
<h2>When to choose private care instead</h2>
<p>Via ferrata days, very long hikes, severe heat, winter conditions or routes with difficult terrain may be better enjoyed separately. That does not mean your dog gets a boring day.</p>
<p>Nani Pup offers selected private care in Kranjska Gora and a Dog Adventure Day for suitable dogs: a safe, tailored outing built around the dog’s age, fitness, temperament and the weather.</p>
